0f393709371718538347f27c58517a2e20e67c27ba7e21/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e37f47-83d1-5088-9b85-0e2e24287bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342305} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac14869-5cac-504f-add3-8e9d269fda7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342328} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae148b-fb50-5f08-a2c0-8a72efe39137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343656} {:answer/id #uuid "a2572c76-84e6-542d-8804-715f0e7d0f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343665} {:answer/id #uuid "eed4f8df-a5a6-55f1-8a77-0e82d4221bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eed4f8df-a5a6-55f1-8a77-0e82d4221bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344337} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f0f7cf8-f8e0-5481-a04a-8c128f9e6dec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344692}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a42a0c64-6afa-5778-a107-25576497d815" :question/math-academy-id "q-76186" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y-1}$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em},$ for $t\\in [\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{6}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$, then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=t+1.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y-1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{(t+1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={u}^{3/2}{|}_{9}^{25} \\\\  & ={25}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2} \\\\  & =125-27 \\\\  & =98\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4206dd2-7f0d-5a39-81ac-42f0f7afd440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(\\sqrt{2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133111} {:answer/id #uuid "bef21a17-bc41-51cb-bd6f-ccfb84019bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133112} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e019f4-70de-5a92-a4ca-84b324f58dcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{2} + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133113} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9f6d66-d570-59cd-b849-c86b67cd5b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133114} {:answer/id #uuid "721bd124-318f-520e-bd5c-2af024eb5c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(2\\sqrt{2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "721bd124-318f-520e-bd5c-2af024eb5c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(2\\sqrt{2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133115} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc97926-178c-5cd1-8a7d-3f12cff0ecfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133110}] :question/id #uuid "04dc89d7-54ea-5f6e-88f3-7fb468d7a9b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76187" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4yz\\cos xds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}\\hspace{0.167em}$ for $0 < t < \\frac{π}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "Here $x=t$, $y=\\sin t$, and $z=1$. The speed is $\\sqrt{1+\\cos^2t}$. Thus $$\\int_C4yz\\cos x\\,ds=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}4\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{1+\\cos^2t}\\,dt.$$ Set $u=1+\\cos^2t$, so $du=-2\\sin t\\cos t\\,dt$. The integral becomes $$2\\int_1^2u^{1/2}\\,du=\\frac43(2\\sqrt2-1).$$ The omitted endpoints have finite limits and do not change this value."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c74ebde-4483-557d-bc7f-96d2eae35d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281803} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1f1600-c667-5053-87bf-a32e6404cd47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282216} {:answer/id #uuid "8298ffad-58f2-5c03-8c84-d9f00f0346bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7(2\\sqrt{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282818} {:answer/id #uuid "c84c1cb8-787a-5d55-a741-7cdf8092bf77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283442} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bd9128-7071-5f60-80ca-f9fcc77741a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c1f1600-c667-5053-87bf-a32e6404cd47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282216} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c701af0-373b-5ef2-ba21-ba5e8d9c684f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284116}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2dda2ab-f3d6-5a31-a33f-5b15f55a34c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167612" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em},$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$, then along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y={t}^{3}.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(3t)^{3}+{t}^{3} \\\\  & =27{t}^{3}+{t}^{3} \\\\  & =28{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}28{t}^{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}84{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{4},$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}84{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}21\\cdot {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =14({2}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec86e50-8126-5c8c-9c9e-7b518ae7992e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342364} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8f2e2d-51c7-5375-9e3c-dedb16da721e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343311} {:answer/id #uuid "a735d400-c4c3-527f-96ac-bc42ae20a5ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343710} {:answer/id #uuid "adb27abf-79df-56ff-bf2b-b4946023e074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343766} {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb48e-7c9d-51a8-92b1-9e4b43f6a2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb48e-7c9d-51a8-92b1-9e4b43f6a2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343969} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75af4f4-1064-571f-8598-a6fa76be3720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344838}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d73cf87-1a19-5cca-88b7-764818d04cb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76479" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  along the curve $C$ parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $0<t<\\sqrt{\\pi /2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=-{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}|t| \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ because $t>0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.167em}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4b14bf-da94-5587-9476-c6c9f1763513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342466} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd66d9f-895b-589b-a593-4fcc58493314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342486} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee36c09-cb9f-5764-9a87-cf5b7c3eff99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342494} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3d5513-4913-5c07-b97e-b502cd923def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342600} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5cec4d-eafc-5470-8930-282ed398fa90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ee36c09-cb9f-5764-9a87-cf5b7c3eff99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342494} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e475247-06f5-5122-b5e7-edfc19026889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344627}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ffd2389-f864-5810-9cd3-9a41b1f9dc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-76484" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5},$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$   \n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}+9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =5|\\operatorname{cos}t| \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\ge 0$ when $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{16{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{5} \\\\  & =5{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =25{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =25\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =25(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b563221-3548-560c-9fc8-b290090582fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342472} {:answer/id #uuid "34a3d682-f094-5b8e-9966-be8b9fc8ad8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342675} {:answer/id #uuid "618e4548-1271-521c-9a14-4e3337de95f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343065} {:answer/id #uuid "cd843b1d-6720-54f4-98b0-8e4c42afe883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344048} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f98bc6-232d-5249-b4a6-3f2a6e7f4860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4f98bc6-232d-5249-b4a6-3f2a6e7f4860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344249} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf920228-2dc2-55b3-a8f5-efbc9659d941" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344829}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "004b67de-a618-52fc-b8e0-1a45d41d2be5" :question/math-academy-id "q-76496" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\nfor $1<t<\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},\n$$ then along the curve we have $x=t,$ $y=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},$ and $z=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}}{t}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+2{t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=4t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({5}^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125c9fef-e1e6-59ab-a694-d2223cf1b6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "258" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536175} {:answer/id #uuid "632784b4-300a-57d6-8fbd-990ee92f3666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536196} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9d9f69-d003-56a1-8175-bb385c5221a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{513}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536211} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f82d33-be42-5959-a84f-d0438d80b642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536224} {:answer/id #uuid "e15c611b-dde5-5f8a-b2e4-d0b0be4d5705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c9d9f69-d003-56a1-8175-bb385c5221a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{513}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536211} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac87f4f-c6c9-51ac-93c4-d5e8ee44e66b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536174}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "353d7359-1a70-56e3-a4d7-6e5a53fc624b" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=9 y$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2},t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\sqrt{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=t$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9 y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(9)(t) \\\\  & =9 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(9 t)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(8 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}9 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{49}\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{1}^{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[(49)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[(343)-(1)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1029}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{513}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01df4532-b45d-53b7-a427-d6227daed514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536171} {:answer/id #uuid "859def66-0165-5571-8ffa-7f3709f9ec2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536206} {:answer/id #uuid "a48a6739-81cb-508a-b07a-3a3ab0634724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536214} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cecb10-b5ce-5055-ae8b-93d6718db0ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536227} {:answer/id #uuid "dc68bb0d-38dd-5ebb-8d67-8a4394694daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc68bb0d-38dd-5ebb-8d67-8a4394694daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536230} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9c97da8-c43e-5d5d-9311-e6206eff1054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536237}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3aeb7177-27b3-5c11-a4d0-3497fd32abae" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=3 y$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2},t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=t$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3 y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3)(t) \\\\  & =3 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(3 t)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(8 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}3 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(27)-(1)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{27}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0494b507-6893-5677